/**
@mainpage bstream: binary I/O in C++20

bstream is a header-only library for typed binary reads and writes, memory-backed
standard streams, endian conversion, and bit fields. Include
`<bstream/bstream.hh>` for the complete API or an individual component header.
Byte readers and writers default to little endian. Bit readers and writers
default to most-significant-bit first; byte order and bit order are independent.

@section start Quick start
This example writes into caller-owned storage and reads the serialized bytes.
`require()` in the examples checks a condition and throws if it is false.
@snippet usage.cc span_roundtrip

@section choose Choose an interface
| Task | Interface |
|------|-----------|
| Read existing contiguous memory | bstream::span_reader |
| Write into a fixed buffer | bstream::span_writer |
| Read/write standard streams or binary files | bstream::stream_reader, bstream::stream_writer |
| Pack/unpack bit fields in memory | bstream::bit_writer, bstream::bit_reader |
| Pack/unpack bit fields on standard streams | bstream::bit_stream_writer, bstream::bit_stream_reader |
| Present memory as a standard stream | bstream::span_istream, bstream::span_ostream |
| Mirror input or broadcast output | bstream::tee_input_stream, bstream::tee_output_stream |
| Copy a stream to another destination | bstream::stream_copier |
| Convert scalar or array byte order | bstream::byte_order and the free functions in byte_order.hh |

@section contents Guides
- @ref setup
- @ref serialization
- @ref bit_guide
- @ref stream_adapters
- @ref error_guide
- @ref usage_source

@section lifetime Ownership and concurrency
Span readers, span writers, memory streams, and their returned views borrow
storage. Keep the storage alive and at a stable address for every operation and
for the lifetime of each returned view. Resizing a backing vector can invalidate
those views. A reader constructed from a temporary container immediately risks
dangling storage. Returned strings and vectors own their decoded data.

Stream wrappers borrow their streams; they do not open, close, or own files.
Open file streams with `std::ios::binary` to avoid platform text translation.
The bit_writer constructor taking only a bit_order owns its output vector;
its other constructors borrow a vector, span, or span_writer. Tee streams also
borrow every destination. Copying a span_reader or span_writer copies its cursor
and byte order while sharing storage; copying a stream_reader or stream_writer
shares the same underlying stream position. tee_stream_buf is neither copyable
nor movable because its input cursors refer to its internal storage.
Instances maintain mutable cursors and buffers and provide no synchronization.
Use separate instances and storage, or synchronize access externally.

@section format Format portability
Prefer fixed-width integer types and an explicit byte order in file formats.
Containers have no implicit element count, and aggregate padding is never
serialized by PFR. Integral and enum values use their C++ object representation;
endianness conversion does not normalize widths or `bool` between platforms.
Floating-point serialization and endian conversion accept only IEEE 754
binary32 and binary64 representations. Padded/extended and binary128
`long double` representations are rejected at compile time: returning reversed
bytes as a floating-point value can lose data. A binary64 `long double`, as on
MSVC, is supported. Use raw byte I/O and an explicit format for other representations.
Only feed valid representations to
typed reads. The implementation assumes native scalar order is little or big
endian, and does not support a mixed-endian native representation.
*/

/** @page setup Building, configuration, and documentation
@section cmake_setup CMake consumer
Link `neutrino::bstream` after adding this repository with `add_subdirectory` or
`FetchContent_MakeAvailable`. The interface target requests C++20 and propagates
the enabled dependencies. GCC, Clang, MSVC, and clang-cl are covered by the
compiler CI configuration; Windows jobs use Visual Studio 2022.

An installed package exposes the same target through
`find_package(bstream 0.1 CONFIG REQUIRED)`. Enable `NEUTRINO_BSTREAM_INSTALL`,
then run `cmake --install build --prefix /path/to/prefix`. Headers and CMake
package files follow `CMAKE_INSTALL_INCLUDEDIR` and `CMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR`;
the prefix can be relocated. Set `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` in consumers to locate it.
The package preserves its configured optional features. Enabled dependencies
must be available as installed `failsafe` and Boost 1.83+ (`pfr` component)
packages, or as existing `neutrino::failsafe` and `Boost::pfr` targets. To install
without those requirements, configure both `BSTREAM_WITH_FAILSAFE` and
`BSTREAM_WITH_BOOST_PFR` to `OFF`. bstream does not bundle dependency headers.

| Option | Default | Effect |
|--------|---------|--------|
| `BSTREAM_WITH_BOOST_PFR` | `ON` | Fetch/link Boost.PFR for supported aggregates |
| `BSTREAM_WITH_FAILSAFE` | `ON` | Fetch/link failsafe for expected-result APIs |
| `NEUTRINO_BSTREAM_BUILD_DOCS` | `OFF` | Enable the `bstream_docs` target; requires Doxygen 1.9.8+ |
| `NEUTRINO_BSTREAM_BUILD_EXAMPLES` | Top-level native build: `ON` | Build `bstream_usage_examples` independently of the tests |
| `NEUTRINO_BSTREAM_BUILD_TESTS` | Top-level native build: `ON` | Build tests, standalone header checks, and usage examples |
| `NEUTRINO_BSTREAM_INSTALL` | Top-level build: `ON` | Install headers and the relocatable `neutrino::bstream` CMake target |
| `NEUTRINO_WARNINGS_AS_ERRORS` | `ON` | Treat enabled warnings in project verification targets as errors |

For direct header inclusion, add `include/` to the compiler include path and
enable C++20. Boost.PFR and failsafe are detected from available headers.
Defining `BSTREAM_WITH_BOOST_PFR=0` disables reflection even if Boost is present.
`BSTREAM_HAS_PFR` and `BSTREAM_HAS_FAILSAFE` indicate the APIs that were detected;
do not define these detection macros yourself. Disabling the failsafe CMake
option stops fetching/linking it, but an otherwise visible failsafe installation
can still be detected by the headers. Use consistent configuration across
translation units.

Every public header includes the internal Windows configuration header. It
defines `NOMINMAX` on Windows and removes existing `min`/`max` macros, including
when Windows headers were included first. No project-wide define is needed.

@section build_docs Generate this reference
@code{.sh}
cmake -S . -B build-docs -DNEUTRINO_BSTREAM_BUILD_DOCS=ON
cmake --build build-docs --target bstream_docs
@endcode
Open `build-docs/docs/reference/html/index.html`. XML is generated alongside
the HTML for tooling. Missing public documentation, malformed commands,
unresolved links, and incomplete parameter documentation fail the docs build.
Internal `bstream::detail` implementation types are excluded from the reference.

@section run_examples Compile and run the examples
@code{.sh}
cmake -S . -B build-examples -DNEUTRINO_BSTREAM_BUILD_EXAMPLES=ON
cmake --build build-examples --config Release --target bstream_usage_examples
@endcode
Run `build-examples/bin/bstream_usage_examples` for a single-configuration
generator, or `build-examples/bin/Release/bstream_usage_examples.exe` with Visual
Studio. When tests are enabled, CTest runs the examples too. Optional examples
are compiled only when their dependency is available. The reference always
shows the optional APIs and states their availability.
*/

/** @page serialization Byte streams and serialization
@section memory_parsing Bounded parsing and zero-copy slices
span_reader checks available bytes before each raw read, skip, and slice.
read_span() and subreader() advance the parent cursor and borrow the same
storage; subreader() inherits the parent's byte order and has its own cursor.
@snippet usage.cc slicing

@section stream_serialization Standard streams and containers
@snippet usage.cc streams
Vectors and arrays serialize elements in order, without a length prefix.
`reader >> vector` fills its existing size and never resizes it;
`read_vector<T>(count)` allocates the requested element count. Scalar containers
are read in bulk and converted through byte_order.hh. `std::vector<bool>` is not
a contiguous scalar buffer and is not supported by the bulk reader overloads.
Validate externally supplied lengths before allocating containers or strings.
Reading a non-scalar container requires a matching extraction operator for
its elements (or supported aggregate reflection).

@section string_formats String formats
| Writer / reader | Encoding |
|-----------------|----------|
| `write_cstring` / `read_cstring` | String bytes followed by a zero byte; embedded zeros terminate reading |
| `write_fixed_string` / `read_fixed_string` | Exactly the specified width; writer truncates or pads; reader consumes the whole width |
| `write_pascal_string<LenType>` / `read_pascal_string<LenType>` | Length in the configured byte order, then that many string bytes |

Fixed-string reading defaults to returning bytes before the first zero; pass
`false` for `strip_nulls` to preserve all bytes. Pascal prefixes default to
`uint8_t` on both readers and writers. Use matching unsigned integral prefix
types, such as `uint16_t`, for longer strings. Writers reject an unrepresentable
length with parse_error before emitting the prefix. Readers do not validate
negative signed prefixes; select an unsigned prefix for binary formats.
Strings carry bytes without encoding conversion.

@section endian_usage Byte order
@snippet usage.cc byte_order
Both byte readers use byte_order.hh for scalar and batch conversion. A per-call
`read<T, Endian>()` or `write<T, Endian>()` temporarily overrides the configured
order for all nested fields and restores it on return or exception.

@section reflected_records Aggregate records (optional Boost.PFR)
@snippet usage.cc aggregates
Supported aggregates are serialized field by field in declaration order;
the wire size is the sum of the serialized fields, not `sizeof(record)`.
PFR eligibility is determined by the adapter and Boost.PFR's supported aggregate
forms. This is not raw-memory serialization of arbitrary C++ objects.
`read<T>()` decodes field values before constructing the record, allowing const
value members, including nested records. Extracting into an existing record
with `operator>>` still requires it to be assignable. Reference members are not
supported by automatic aggregate deserialization.

@section custom_records Custom operators
Use matching extraction/insertion operators for a non-scalar type that is not
selected for PFR reflection. This non-aggregate example works with PFR on or off.
@snippet usage.cc custom_type
@snippet usage.cc custom_records
Custom operators must consume/produce the intended fields and propagate errors.
A read constructs `T{}` before invoking custom extraction, so that path requires
a default-constructible type. Aggregate reflection takes precedence over custom
operators when `read<T>()` / `write<T>()` selects a reflectable aggregate.
*/

/** @page bit_guide Bit fields
@section packed_memory Packing and parsing
@snippet usage.cc bits
Counts from 0 through 64 are accepted at any bit offset. A zero-bit read or peek
returns zero, and a zero-bit write is a no-op. Reads return a right-aligned value;
writes use only the low `count` bits of their input.

With `msb_first`, the next physical bit is the high remaining bit of the byte
and the first bit becomes the high bit of the requested field. With `lsb_first`,
the next physical bit is the low remaining bit and the first bit becomes bit 0
of the result. Bit order does not inherit a byte reader/writer's endian setting.

@section bit_alignment Peeking, alignment, and state
Peeking can fetch underlying bytes but does not consume logical bits. Alignment
discards only the rest of the current input byte, retaining prefetched whole
bytes. On output, it emits the pending byte with zero padding by default;
any nonzero `pad_bit` requests one bits. Change bit order only at a byte boundary,
or set the already active order; another change throws bstream_error.

`bytes_remaining()` on bit_reader counts bytes not yet fetched, whereas
`total_bits_remaining()` also counts buffered bits. Output counters count
accepted payload bits and successful byte writes, excluding alignment padding.
bit_stream_writer's bulk write_bytes() updates its counter only if the entire
bulk write succeeds; a failed underlying stream can still have emitted a prefix.

@section streaming_bits Bit fields on standard streams
@snippet usage.cc bit_streams
Do not interleave direct stream reads with a bit reader that holds buffered
bytes. Likewise, flush a bit writer before bypassing it to write directly.
Input has no automatic way to distinguish payload bits from final-byte padding;
the format must supply a field count or length.

The owning bit_writer returns its vector through finish(). Other modes borrow
their destination: inspect data() after flush(), or access that destination.
data() excludes the pending partial byte and includes any pre-existing bytes
in an external vector or span_writer. Treat finish() as the end of an owning
writer's session. Destructors attempt a flush but suppress failures; call
flush() or finish() explicitly when delivery must be checked.
Moving a bit_writer preserves its destination and pending bits in the new owner.
The source exposes an empty data() view and zero counters; it can be destroyed
or assigned a new writer.
*/

/** @page stream_adapters Memory streams, tee streams, and copying
@section memory_adapters Fixed memory through standard streams
@snippet usage.cc memory_streams
Memory streams never grow their backing storage. Writes beyond capacity set
stream failure state (or throw according to the standard stream exception
mask). Seeking is bounded by the entire buffer capacity. `chars_written()` and
the written views reflect the current put position, not a historical high-water
mark: seeking backward shortens those views. reset() rewinds and clears stream
flags without erasing bytes. Empty buffers support tell/seek at position zero;
nonzero seeks and unavailable input/output modes still fail. Read-only buffers
reject a putback character that differs from the character previously read.
span_streambuf move operations preserve separate
read/write positions and leave the source with no usable storage.

@section tee_adapters Broadcasting and mirroring
@snippet usage.cc tee_output
@snippet usage.cc tee_input
An output tee forwards bytes to registered destinations in order. A destination
failure is reported through the tee's stream state; bytes accepted by earlier
destinations are not rolled back. An exception from a destination can stop
delivery to later destinations. An output tee with no destinations is a sink.

An input tee reads and mirrors chunks of up to 4096 bytes. A peek or one-byte
read can therefore advance the underlying stream and audit outputs by an entire
chunk before the caller consumes it. Adding a mirror later does not replay
already fetched bytes. Flush destinations explicitly when required.

@section copy_helpers Copy semantics
stream_copier starts at the current input position. String/vector destinations
are appended to, never cleared. The default scratch buffer is 8192 bytes on the
stack; larger requested chunks allocate heap storage. A zero chunk copies zero
bytes. Buffered output-copy failures throw bstream_error, while ordinary input
EOF/failure stops copying; inspect the input stream to distinguish failures from
EOF. copy_unbuffered() stops on either stream's failure and returns the number
of successfully transferred bytes. Standard stream exception masks still apply.
No copy helper flushes the output destination.
*/

/** @page error_guide Errors and recovery
@section exceptions Exceptions
bstream_error derives from `std::runtime_error`. eof_error reports bounded
buffer exhaustion or insufficient input; parse_error reports a rejected format
value such as a Pascal length that cannot fit its prefix. Other stream-write,
flush, and bit-count/order failures use bstream_error.
@snippet usage.cc errors

A failed bounded scalar/raw span read leaves its cursor unchanged. A streaming
read can consume a prefix before failing. Compound operations (records,
containers, strings, and writes) are not transactions: earlier fields or bytes
may have been committed when a later step fails. A failed span Pascal read can
already have consumed its length prefix. Bit refills can fetch bytes before an
EOF exception without consuming the requested logical field. Failed bit output
retains valid pending state, but replaying a whole failed write can duplicate a
prefix that was already accepted. There is no universal rollback guarantee.

Underlying standard streams can throw `std::ios_base::failure` according to their
exception masks; allocation and user-defined operator exceptions can propagate
as well. `seek()` on stream_reader/stream_writer clears stream flags before
seeking; `skip()` does not. Stream peeking requires a seekable input and only
restores position after a successful read and successful seek. Stream `tell()`
returns zero when position lookup fails, which is indistinguishable from offset
zero without checking the underlying stream state.

@section expected_results Expected results (optional failsafe)
When `BSTREAM_HAS_FAILSAFE` is defined, byte readers expose `try_read<T>()`, and
bit_stream_reader exposes `try_read_bit()` and `try_read_bits()`.
@snippet usage.cc expected
These wrappers return bstream::expected with the caught `std::exception` message
as a string on failure. They retain the underlying read's partial-progress
semantics. The wrappers are `noexcept`: a non-standard exception, or allocation
failure while constructing the error result, terminates rather than returning
an error. They are not a general exception barrier for arbitrary custom code.
*/

